# Domain 5.0 Security Fundamentals: Study Notes (15%)

## 1. Threats, vulnerabilities and mitigation

Key terms:

| Term | Meaning | House analogy |
|---|---|---|
| Asset | Anything of value (data, devices, reputation) | Valuables inside |
| Vulnerability | A weakness | Unlocked window |
| Threat | Potential danger that could use a weakness | Burglar |
| Exploit | The method or tool that uses a vulnerability | Crowbar, or the act of climbing in |
| Risk | Likelihood × impact | Chance of burglary × cost of loss |
| Mitigation | Control that reduces risk | Lock, alarm, guard |

CIA triad: Confidentiality (only authorized people read it), Integrity (it isn't altered), Availability (it works when needed).

Common threats:

| Threat | What it is |
|---|---|
| Virus | Needs a host file and user action |
| Worm | Self-replicates across the network |
| Trojan | Malware disguised as legitimate software |
| Ransomware | Encrypts data and demands payment |
| Spyware / rootkit | Steals data / hides deep in the OS |
| Phishing | Fake emails; spear = targeted, whaling = executives, vishing = voice, smishing = SMS |
| Social engineering | Manipulating people: pretexting, tailgating, baiting |
| Reconnaissance | Ping sweeps and port scans before an attack |
| DoS / DDoS | Overloads a service; DDoS uses many sources (botnet) |
| Spoofing | Faking an identity (IP, MAC, ARP, DNS) |
| MITM | Attacker sits between two parties |
| Password attacks | Brute force, dictionary |
| Zero-day | Exploit for a flaw with no patch yet |

Layer 2 attacks (covered in section 5): MAC flooding, DHCP starvation and rogue DHCP server, ARP poisoning, VLAN hopping.

Mitigation layers (defense in depth): patching, antivirus/EDR, firewall and IPS, segmentation (VLANs, ACLs), least privilege, backups, MFA, user training, logging and monitoring.

Analogy: a bank vault. The street, the lobby, the counter, the vault door and the safe deposit box each have their own lock, so beating one layer doesn't open everything.

Control categories: preventive (stop), detective (notice), corrective (fix). By type: administrative (policy), technical (firewall), physical (lock).

