## 4. ACLs

Rules:
- Processed top-down, first match wins.
- Every ACL ends with an implicit deny any.
- One ACL per protocol, per direction, per interface.
- Inbound filters before routing. Outbound filters after routing.
- ACLs don't filter traffic that the router itself originates.

Analogy: a gate guard with a guest list checked line by line. The first matching line decides, and anyone not on the list is turned away.

| Type | Number ranges | Matches | Place |
|---|---|---|---|
| Standard | 1-99, 1300-1999 | Source IP only | Close to destination |
| Extended | 100-199, 2000-2699 | Protocol, source, destination, ports | Close to source |
| Named | Name | Either type, editable by sequence number | Same as above |

Wildcard mask: 0 = must match, 1 = ignore. Wildcard = 255.255.255.255 minus the subnet mask.

| Network | Wildcard |
|---|---|
| /24 | 0.0.0.255 |
| /26 | 0.0.0.63 |
| Single host | `host` or 0.0.0.0 |
| Any | `any` or 255.255.255.255 |

Configuration:
```
! Standard
ip access-list standard ADMIN-ONLY
 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255

! Extended: block one host from a web server, permit everything else
ip access-list extended BLOCK-WEB
 10 deny tcp host 10.1.1.10 host 10.2.2.20 eq 80
 20 permit ip any any
interface g0/0
 ip access-group BLOCK-WEB in

! Restrict VTY access
line vty 0 15
 access-class ADMIN-ONLY in
```

Edit: insert with a sequence number (`15 deny ...`), remove with `no 20`.

Verify: `show access-lists` (shows hit counters), `show ip interface g0/0` (shows the applied ACL), `show running-config | section access`.

IPv6: `ipv6 access-list NAME` and `ipv6 traffic-filter NAME in`. Implicit rules permit neighbor discovery before the final deny.

Traps:
- A missing `permit ip any any` blocks everything else.
- Specific entries go before general ones.
- An ACL that is created but not applied does nothing.

