## 6. AAA

| Function | Question | Analogy at an airport |
|---|---|---|
| Authentication | Who are you? | Passport check |
| Authorization | What can you do? | Boarding pass decides your gate and lounge |
| Accounting | What did you do? | CCTV and logbook |

| | RADIUS | TACACS+ |
|---|---|---|
| Standard | Open (IETF) | Cisco proprietary |
| Transport | UDP 1812 (auth), 1813 (acct) | TCP 49 |
| Encryption | Password only | Entire payload |
| AAA design | Authentication and authorization combined | Separated |
| Best for | Network access (802.1X, VPN, Wi-Fi) | Device administration, per-command authorization |

Configuration (RADIUS):
```
aaa new-model
username admin secret AdminPass          ! local fallback
radius server RAD1
 address ipv4 10.1.1.50 auth-port 1812 acct-port 1813
 key SharedSecret
aaa authentication login default group radius local
aaa authorization exec default group radius local
aaa accounting exec default start-stop group radius
```

TACACS+ uses `tacacs server TAC1`, `address ipv4 ...` and `key ...`, with `group tacacs+` in the method lists.

Method lists: methods are tried in order. The router falls to `local` only if the server does not respond, not if the server rejects the login.

802.1X roles:
- Supplicant is the client.
- Authenticator is the switch, AP or WLC.
- Authentication server is RADIUS (for example Cisco ISE).
- EAP carries the credentials.

Traps:
- `aaa new-model` takes effect immediately, so create a local user first or you can lock yourself out.
- Keep `local` as a fallback method.
- Use TACACS+ for command-level control of admins.

